    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;36)</div>\n<p>Prior to the publication of Thomas Kuhn's <i>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</i> in 1962, the dominant philosophy of science&mdash;cultivated by logical positivists of the Vienna Circle and orthodox empiricists&mdash;conceived of scientific progress as a steady, cumulative accretion of verified empirical facts and refined inductive generalizations. In this textbook narrative, science was an objective, linear march toward transcendent metaphysical truth, systematically discarding obsolete superstitions in favor of mathematically verified laws. Working as a historian of physics, Kuhn dismantled this triumphant historiography, demonstrating that historical scientific practice reveals not continuous incremental growth, but an episodic succession of conservative consolidation punctuated by radical ruptures that shatter the conceptual foundations of an entire discipline.</p>\n<p>Kuhn observed that disciplines originate in a &ldquo;pre-paradigmatic&rdquo; phase, wherein rival schools compete without consensus, continually debating fundamental assumptions. Maturity arrives with the establishment of a &ldquo;paradigm&rdquo;&mdash;a comprehensive constellation of theoretical commitments, laboratory instrumentation, metaphysical axioms, and concrete problem-solutions (&ldquo;exemplars&rdquo;) shared by a specialized scientific community. Pedagogical training socializes graduate apprentices into these exemplars (such as the Newtonian harmonic oscillator), conditioning them to perceive nature through the paradigm's conceptual lenses. Once a paradigm achieves hegemony, scientists engage in what Kuhn terms &ldquo;normal science.&rdquo; Far from executing bold attempts to refute reigning theory, normal scientists conduct disciplined &ldquo;puzzle-solving.&rdquo; They calibrate laboratory instruments, determine physical constants with heightened precision, and articulate existing theoretical principles to encompass marginal empirical domains. During normal science, anomalies&mdash;experimental results that clash with theoretical expectations&mdash;are routinely dismissed as laboratory artifacts, instrumental defects, or researcher error. However, when persistent anomalies accumulate, resist resolution across multiple experimental designs, and afflict foundational tenets, the discipline enters an acute &ldquo;crisis.&rdquo; Professional consensus collapses, philosophical debates reignite, and rival speculative factions proliferate.</p>\n<p>The resolution of a crisis arrives through a &ldquo;scientific revolution&rdquo;&mdash;the non-cumulative overthrow of the established paradigm by an incompatible successor. Kuhn's most radical contention was that competing paradigms are fundamentally &ldquo;incommensurable.&rdquo; Because there exists no neutral, theory-independent observation language or mechanical algorithm to arbitrate between them, rival paradigms cannot be directly measured against a common standard. Kuhn identified three interconnected dimensions of incommensurability: semantic, where shared terminology undergoes radical conceptual drift (for instance, the term &ldquo;mass&rdquo; designates an invariant intrinsic property in Newtonian physics, but a velocity-dependent quantity in Einsteinian relativity); observational, where the &ldquo;theory-ladenness&rdquo; of perception causes rival scientists to literally see different phenomena when observing the same apparatus (an Aristotelian sees a constrained falling stone where Galileo sees a pendulum with weight and tension); and evaluative, where paradigms disagree over which questions constitute pressing scientific problems and what criteria establish an acceptable explanation. Consequently, paradigm conversion cannot be compelled by deductive logic alone; it resembles an all-at-once perceptual &ldquo;gestalt shift&rdquo; or conversion experience.</p>\n<p>Kuhn's incommensurability thesis ignited fierce resistance from philosophers of science like Karl Popper and Imre Lakatos, who accused Kuhn of reducing scientific progress to irrational relativism and &ldquo;mob psychology.&rdquo; If revolutionary shifts are not dictated by objective evidence, critics warned, science loses its privileged epistemic authority. In his 1969 Postscript, Kuhn sought to temper these extreme interpretations, clarifying that incommensurability does not entail total incomparability. Scientists debating rival paradigms share broad epistemic values&mdash;such as predictive accuracy, internal consistency, broad explanatory scope, simplicity, and fruitfulness. However, because these values operate as flexible maxims rather than rigid decision algorithms, individual scientists can legitimately prioritize different values, ensuring that revolutionary transitions remain protracted, interpretive human struggles rather than mechanical computational calculations.</p>",
