    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In his 1934 masterpiece, <i>The Logic of Scientific Discovery</i>, Karl Popper formulated what he considered the foundational challenge of epistemology: the &ldquo;demarcation problem&rdquo;&mdash;the quest to draw a sharp, objective dividing line between genuine empirical science and pseudo-science, mathematics, and metaphysics. At the time, the dominant logical positivists of the Vienna Circle championed verificationism, asserting that a proposition is scientifically meaningful if and only if it can be empirically confirmed by inductive observation. Popper mounted a devastating critique against this consensus, arguing that verificationism rests upon an untenable reliance on inductive inference. Channeling David Hume's famous skeptical critique of induction, Popper demonstrated that no finite series of positive empirical observations can logically validate a universal law. No matter how many millions of white swans an ornithologist catalogs, one cannot logically deduce the conclusion that &ldquo;all swans are white,&rdquo; because future empirical discoveries remain open to contradictory counterexamples.</p>\n<p>To rescue scientific rationality from the inductive impasse, Popper proposed a purely deductive alternative: <i>falsifiability</i> (or refutability). Popper's central insight rests upon an inescapable logical asymmetry between verification and falsification. While no number of confirming observations can ever logically prove a universal hypothesis true, a single reproducible counter-instance (such as observing a single black swan) deductively proves the universal proposition false via <i>modus tollens</i>. Therefore, Popper argued, a theoretical system qualifies as scientific if and only if it makes bold, risky predictions that categorically prohibit specific empirical outcomes. A genuine scientific theory partitions the universe of possible observations into two non-empty sets: those phenomena it permits, and those it strictly forbids (its &ldquo;potential falsifiers&rdquo;). The broader the range of forbidden phenomena, the greater the theory's empirical content and vulnerability to experimental refutation.</p>\n<p>Popper deployed falsificationism to mount a scathing indictment of intellectual paradigms that commanded immense prestige in early twentieth-century Vienna, most notably Freudian psychoanalysis, Adlerian individual psychology, and orthodox Marxist historicism. Popper observed that adherents of these systems possessed an uncanny ability to interpret literally every conceivable human behavior or political event as a verification of their theories. If a man pushes a child into a river to drown, Freudian psychoanalysts explain the crime via repressed psychosexual trauma; if another man dives into the river to save the child, analysts explain the heroic act via sublimation. Because these doctrines could explain every outcome post-hoc, they were immune to empirical refutation, functioning as dogmatic mythologies masquerading as empirical science. In stark contrast, Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity made a daring, highly risky prediction: that the gravitational field of the sun would bend starlight by an exact quantitative angle. When Arthur Eddington's 1919 eclipse expedition tested this prediction, Einstein's theory stood exposed to the acute risk of immediate refutation.</p>\n<p>Despite its deductive clarity, Popper's falsificationism encounters formidable epistemological and historical objections. Foremost among them is the Duhem-Quine thesis, which demonstrates the inescapable holism of empirical testing. An isolated hypothesis is never tested in a vacuum; testing always involves an interconnected web of auxiliary hypotheses, background assumptions, and instrument theories. When an experiment yields a negative result, formal logic dictates only that at least one proposition in the theoretical web is false, but it cannot pinpoint whether the fault lies in the primary hypothesis, a secondary assumption, or a faulty measuring apparatus. Historically, had scientists dogmatically obeyed Popperian falsificationism, foundational theories such as Copernican astronomy and Newtonian gravitation would have been discarded prematurely, as both initially confronted observational anomalies that were later resolved through protective auxiliary adjustments.</p>",
