    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 8 (Questions 43&ndash;48)</div>\n<p>During the latter half of the twentieth century, breathtaking advancements in neuroimaging, molecular neurobiology, and computational cognitive science fueled the orthodox physicalist assumption that subjective human consciousness would soon be fully explained in terms of cortical neurochemistry. Just as thermodynamics had been smoothly reduced to statistical mechanics and heredity to the molecular structure of DNA, materialists anticipated that the mysteries of the mind would dissolve once neural firing patterns and synaptic plasticity were meticulously mapped. However, philosophers such as Thomas Nagel and Joseph Levine exposed a stubborn conceptual barrier: the &ldquo;explanatory gap.&rdquo; Physical science is exquisitely equipped to explain physical structures, biochemical mechanisms, and behavioral dynamics. Yet there remains an impassable chasm between objective neural processes&mdash;such as sodium-potassium ionic flux across axonal membranes&mdash;and the first-person, felt qualities of subjective experience, known to philosophers as <i>qualia</i>.</p>\n<p>In his 1995 paper and subsequent treatise <i>The Conscious Mind</i>, Australian philosopher David Chalmers transformed this debate by demarcating consciousness research into the &ldquo;easy problems&rdquo; and the &ldquo;Hard Problem.&rdquo; The easy problems of consciousness, Chalmers emphasizes, are conceptually straightforward even if neurobiologically daunting: they involve deciphering the functional and computational mechanisms of the brain. Explaining how the brain discriminates sensory stimuli, integrates information across cortical regions, focuses attention, or produces verbal reports all represent functional problems amenable to standard cognitive science. In stark contrast stands the Hard Problem: why should the physical execution of these cognitive functions be accompanied by subjective inner experience at all? Why doesn't information processing occur entirely &ldquo;in the dark,&rdquo; without any subjective feeling of what it is like to see the azure of a summer sky or experience the sting of grief?</p>\n<p>To demonstrate that subjective consciousness cannot be logically deduced from physical facts alone, Chalmers advanced the controversial thought experiment of the &ldquo;philosophical zombie&rdquo; (<i>p-zombie</i>). A p-zombie is an entity physically and molecularly identical to an ordinary human being down to the finest subatomic particle: its brain possesses identical neural wiring, it processes sensory inputs identically, and it demonstrates identical outward behaviors&mdash;flinching when burned and conversing intelligently about philosophy. However, the p-zombie is completely devoid of inner phenomenal life; it experiences no sensations, pleasures, or pains. Chalmers argues that because a world populated by p-zombies is logically conceivable (it entails no internal contradiction), physicalism is necessarily false. If complete physical duplication does not logically guarantee the presence of consciousness, then consciousness cannot simply be identical to physical brain states. This conclusion is reinforced by Frank Jackson's famous &ldquo;Knowledge Argument,&rdquo; wherein Mary, a neuroscientist who knows all physical facts about vision while confined to a monochromatic room, acquires novel knowledge of what red feels like upon encountering an actual red rose.</p>\n<p>Confronted with the explanatory gap, Chalmers proposes a non-reductive &ldquo;naturalistic dualism,&rdquo; arguing that consciousness must be acknowledged as a fundamental, irreducible feature of the universe, on par with mass, space-time, and electromagnetic charge. Yet reductive physicalists have mounted an aggressive counteroffensive. Philosophers such as Daniel Dennett and Patricia Churchland contend that Chalmers' Hard Problem is a seductive illusion generated by philosophical confusion. Under Dennett's &ldquo;illusionism,&rdquo; qualia do not exist as independent, non-physical properties; rather, they are merely introspective user-illusions produced by the brain's cognitive machinery. Once neuroscience successfully deciphers all the functional mechanisms that Chalmers labels &ldquo;easy,&rdquo; Dennett argues, the supposed Hard Problem will simply evaporate, revealing that there was never any residual metaphysical mystery left to explain.</p>",
