    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 9 (Questions 49&ndash;53)</div>\n<p>In her landmark 2007 treatise, <i>Epistemic Injustice: Power and the Ethics of Knowing</i>, British philosopher Miranda Fricker pioneered a vital intersection between epistemology, moral philosophy, and social theory. For centuries, orthodox Western epistemology operated within an idealized, individualistic paradigm, treating epistemic agents as abstract, interchangeable minds evaluating propositions in a frictionless social vacuum. From Cartesian foundationalism through mid-twentieth-century analytic philosophy, the knowing subject was conceived as deracinated and autonomous, unencumbered by social identities, historical hierarchies, or material power relations. Fricker fundamentally dismantled this bloodless abstraction, demonstrating that social power relations systematically infect everyday cognitive practices, inflicting distinct forms of harm on individuals specifically in their capacity as knowers. Fricker identifies two primary species of this epistemic harm: <i>testimonial injustice</i> and <i>hermeneutical injustice</i>.</p>\n<p>Testimonial injustice occurs when identity prejudice causes a hearer to assign a deflated level of credibility to a speaker's word. The prejudice at work typically tracks negative social stereotypes regarding race, gender, class, or sexual orientation. Fricker illuminates this through Harper Lee's <i>To Kill a Mockingbird</i>, where the all-white Alabama jury assigns zero credibility to the truthful testimony of Tom Robinson, a Black defendant falsely accused of rape, purely because of deeply ingrained racial prejudice. Fricker emphasizes that testimonial injustice inflicts both an epistemic loss (depriving the hearer and community of the truth) and an ethical injury. Specifically, it degrades the speaker's epistemic personhood: rather than being respected as an active &ldquo;informant&rdquo; capable of conveying rational knowledge, the speaker is reduced to a passive &ldquo;source of information,&rdquo; akin to an inanimate gauge or tree rings from which data is extracted.</p>\n<p>In contrast to testimonial injustice, which occurs within interpersonal communicative exchanges, hermeneutical injustice operates at an earlier, structural level of social life. It arises when a society suffers from a deficit in collective interpretative resources, rendering an individual unable to render their significant social experiences intelligible&mdash;either to others or even to themselves&mdash;because the social group to which they belong has been systematically excluded from participating in the creation of shared linguistic and conceptual meanings. Fricker cites the historical experience of women subjected to workplace harassment prior to the coining of the term &ldquo;sexual harassment&rdquo; by feminist activists in the 1970s. Before this conceptual breakthrough entered the collective hermeneutical reservoir, women experiencing predatory workplace coercion felt disoriented, isolated, and self-blaming, unable to articulate their distress while institutions dismissed them as merely &ldquo;difficult&rdquo; or &ldquo;oversensitive.&rdquo; Crucially, hermeneutical injustice requires no individual perpetrator harboring conscious malice; it is an impersonal, structural injustice embedded within the asymmetrical development of collective language and culture.</p>\n<p>To combat these systemic harms, Fricker advocates cultivating reflexive &ldquo;epistemic virtues,&rdquo; such as testimonial justice, wherein hearers consciously reflect upon and correct for implicit identity prejudices that bias their spontaneous credibility judgments. However, Fricker's framework has prompted significant debate among critical race theorists and feminist epistemologists, such as Kristie Dotson and Gaile Pohlhaus Jr. Dotson argues that Fricker's focus on individual virtuous hearers understates the coercive structural phenomenon of &ldquo;testimonial smothering&rdquo;&mdash;a survival strategy in which marginalized speakers proactively silence their own testimony because they anticipate that dominant audiences lack the epistemic competence or willingness to understand it without prejudice. Furthermore, theorists highlight instances of &ldquo;willful hermeneutical ignorance,&rdquo; wherein dominant groups actively resist adopting marginalized conceptual tools. Consequently, contemporary theorists maintain that remedying epistemic injustice requires far more than individual moral reflexivity; it demands dismantling institutional power dynamics and democratizing the production of collective social concepts.</p>",
